\noindent{\bf BOSE-EINSTEIN CONDENSATION} 

\noindent{Contributed by E. H. Lieb (Princeton Univ.), Sept. 15, 1998.} 

Recent experiments on dilute Bose
gases of atoms seem to corroborate a long held belief that
Bose-Einstein condensation occurs in weakly interacting systems.  The
Hamiltonian for such a gas of $N$  particles can often be modeled by $$
H_N = -{\hbar^2 \over 2m}\sum_{j=1}^N \Delta_j + \sum_{1\leq iÇ j\leq
N} W(x_i-x_j) $$ with a repulsive pair potential $W\geq 0$.  If
$W\equiv 0$ then the normalized  symmetric ground state (with periodic
boundary conditions in a large box of volume $V$) is $\Psi (x_1, ...,
x_N) = V^{-N/2}$ and hence satisfies $$ C_\Psi := \int \Psi (x_1, x_2,
..., x_N) \Psi (y_1,x_2, ..., x_N)
         dx_1dy_1dx_2dx_3\cdots dx_N  = V.
$$ Bose-Einstein condensation in the ground state, with interaction
$W$, is the statement that $C_\Psi$, defined as above, satisfies
$C_\Psi \geq  a V$ with $a > 0$, independent of $V$.  

Can this be proved? It can be proved to be true for a gas of point 
hard core bosons on a hypercubic lattice in dimension 2 or more,
but only for density $1/2$.  Even the 
extension of that result to other densities seems to be a hard problem.
See E. H. Lieb, T. Kennedy and S. Shastry, Phys. Rev. Lett. {\bf 61},
2582 (1988).

Some background can be found in E. Lieb,   The Bose Fluid, {\it
Lectures in Theoretical Physics, Vol.  VIIC,} (Boulder summer school),
University of Colorado Press, 175-224 (1965).

